WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:03.360
In Search of Dharma.

2
00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.880
0: Preface.

3
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.560
This book was assembled in an unusual way,

4
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.500
by an unlikely person, in an unlikely
place.

5
00:00:15.674 --> 00:00:19.780
So before starting, the reader is owed an
open account of all three;

6
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.520
not as credentials, but as disclosures:

7
00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.320
where the work comes from, who helped make
it,

8
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:28.600
and, most importantly, how it was actually
built.

9
00:00:30.589 --> 00:00:34.600
Most books that refer to the word 'dharma'
come from one of two places:

10
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.660
a monastery or a university, or somewhere
in their vicinity.

11
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.580
A teacher hands down a lineage received
from their own teacher,

12
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.640
or a scholar maps the territory from a
careful distance.

13
00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.160
This one comes from neither.

14
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.620
It was written by a lifelong anarchist,

15
00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.280
raised on the remote western rim of
Australia:

16
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.040
a naturalised Indonesian now, writing from
the island of Bali.

17
00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.280
It was built on decades of accumulated
reading and hard experience,

18
00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.840
along with a handful of friends scattered
across three continents and,

19
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.300
latterly, dozens of large language models.

20
00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.460
A preface is normally a throat-clearing,

21
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.020
the part most sensible readers skip on the
way to Part 1.

22
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.580
I'd ask you not to skip this one;

23
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.340
I have made it longer than is fashionable
on purpose.

24
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.720
The eight parts that follow make fairly
bold assertions about

25
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.160
where our worldviews and ethics come from,

26
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:32.120
and they reach their conclusions by a route
unusual enough to require explanation.

27
00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:36.900
I think you can weigh a conclusion better
when you can see who helped reach it,

28
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.900
and what was standing behind the person
doing this reaching;

29
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.820
above all, when you can see how it was
reached.

30
00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.520
I start with the people and ideas behind
the work, since I did not do it alone.

31
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.580
Then comes something of my own life and
how it ground the lens.

32
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.220
Then the one idea the whole book argues.

33
00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:01.380
Last, at greatest length, how the book was
actually made: the methods,

34
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.740
the sources, and the disciplines that kept
the process honest.

35
00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.020
But before any of that, two words need
pinning down: dharma, and its plural.

36
00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.040
A note on two words

37
00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.960
By dharma I mean something deliberately
broad.

38
00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.200
Not the Vedic or Buddhist teaching with a
capital D, but a way of living,

39
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.440
a path one walks: what other traditions
have called a dao,

40
00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.300
or simply the Way, and what we might
equally meet

41
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.980
in the unspoken code of a profession or a
subculture.

42
00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.180
A dharma, in my usage,

43
00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:39.740
is whatever holds a life

44
00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:43.420
or a people together by answering the
question of how to live.

45
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.680
I write it in the plural, dharmas, on
purpose, because there is no single Dharma,

46
00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:53.380
only many, none ranked above the rest
before we look.

47
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.680
What marks a dharma off from mere
unexamined custom is

48
00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.040
that it can be held up and known as a
path:

49
00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.700
named, questioned, reformed, and even
recombined and deliberately made anew.

50
00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.400
That capacity is available, not required,

51
00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.040
since most dharmas are absorbed
half-consciously and never feel chosen at all.

52
00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:17.980
The impetus

53
00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:22.860
Ideas are social before anything else.

54
00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.220
They do not come out of nowhere.

55
00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:27.780
The nearest impetus

56
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.560
for this book was a five-day retreat in
the south of England in February 2018.

57
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.220
It was the first of six such gatherings,
held over two years in England,

58
00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:38.880
the Netherlands and Belgium,

59
00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.720
that together made up a long course
entitled Secular Dharma.

60
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.580
The course was run by Bodhi College,

61
00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.800
an organisation founded by Stephen
Batchelor and others,

62
00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:53.120
to study the earliest Buddhist teachings
without the later religious accretions,

63
00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.920
and with Stephen as one of its principal
teachers.

64
00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:59.600
I had come mainly because of his book
After Buddhism,

65
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:03.140
which does something I had been waiting
most of my life for a book to do:

66
00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:05.860
it treats the Buddha not as the founder of
a faith

67
00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:07.740
but as a sharp, practical ethicist,

68
00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.860
and recasts his teaching as a set of tasks
or skills

69
00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.780
to be done rather than a set of truths to
be believed.

70
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.580
For someone who had spent decades allergic
to belief, that was an open door.

71
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.100
But the lasting thing the retreats gave me
was people.

72
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.240
In that course I first met Rupert Bozeat
and Elfie Klinger.

73
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.540
The three of us kept talking long after
the formal programme ended,

74
00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.000
always online, across the distance between
our countries.

75
00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:39.380
I guess we were a sangha in the loosest
possible sense of that word:

76
00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:42.340
a small standing conversation that went on
for years.

77
00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.400
Somewhere along the way we began recording
the conversations

78
00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.640
and putting them on a YouTube channel,

79
00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:53.480
under the deliberately unsure title 'a
secular dharma?', question mark included.

80
00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:55.540
We made around fifty of them.

81
00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.420
They were never polished and were never
going to find a wide audience,

82
00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:00.620
but they were the laboratory

83
00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.520
where much of what is in this book was
first said out loud,

84
00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:05.860
argued over, and knocked into better
shape.

85
00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:09.460
The question this book spends eight parts
chasing,

86
00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.160
whether dharma can be defined at all, grew
up inside those meetings.

87
00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:19.360
Around that small core sits a wider circle
I owe a good deal to.

88
00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:24.220
What I had wandered into was a scattering
of people in several countries,

89
00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.580
all working the same seam without any one
centre.

90
00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:31.500
The publishing end of it, for me, is the
Tuwhiri Project,

91
00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:35.300
a small New Zealand house run by Ramsey
Margolis and built for books

92
00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:39.040
that take ethics seriously and have no
further use for religion.

93
00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:43.500
Around it are others coming at the same
thing from their own directions:

94
00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.520
the Secular Buddhist Network,

95
00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:49.620
and Mike Slott, whose Mindful Solidarity
ties contemplative practice back

96
00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:51.860
to politics; Winton Higgins,

97
00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.900
who wrote the workbook to After Buddhism;

98
00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:56.260
and a scatter of newsletters and reading
groups,

99
00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.820
all of them trying to keep the wisdom
traditions alive

100
00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:02.800
without asking anyone to believe six
impossible things before breakfast.

101
00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:09.020
I will not dress any of these people or
organisations as the source of my argument,

102
00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:12.580
which is mine to answer for and is set out
plainly further on;

103
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:15.560
what they gave me was readers,
counter-arguments,

104
00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:18.380
and the sense that the work was not merely
eccentric.

105
00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:21.200
I am an unsociable person by temperament,

106
00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.620
and I have not always been an easy member
of any of these circles,

107
00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:27.180
but I did not arrive at these ideas alone.

108
00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:31.500
The shape of the lens

109
00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:36.840
Now the warier part: the life behind the
lens.

110
00:06:37.628 --> 00:06:42.060
I include it because a reader has a right
to know the biases built into a writer,

111
00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:44.280
and mine are not the standard-issue ones.

112
00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.860
But I will keep it tied to the only thing
that matters here,

113
00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.980
which is how it shaped the way I see.

114
00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:56.020
I was born in 1957 in Perth, Western
Australia, which is,

115
00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:59.440
by most measures, the most isolated city
on this planet.

116
00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.280
It is closer to Jakarta than to Sydney.

117
00:07:03.044 --> 00:07:08.340
A child on the western rim of Australia
faces, geographically and imaginatively,

118
00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:11.240
not only east towards the Australian
metropolises,

119
00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.740
but north, across the Indian Ocean,

120
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:18.200
toward the vast and crowded archipelago of
maritime Southeast Asia.

121
00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:22.060
The cultural gravity of my childhood
pulled toward Asia long

122
00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.080
before I really understood why.

123
00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.500
Parts of that childhood were genuinely
wonderful.

124
00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:32.760
A working-class upbringing in the northern
suburbs of Perth came

125
00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:36.240
with a kind of unsupervised freedom that
has all but vanished since:

126
00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:41.220
miles of bushland and wetlands to roam,
the run of a whole small world,

127
00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:44.580
the sort of thing almost no child I know
is granted now.

128
00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:46.620
But I did not fit the place.

129
00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:51.540
The Perth of that era thought of itself as
a distant outpost of the British Empire,

130
00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:53.600
and I was an odd, contrary,

131
00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.240
differently wired boy who could not see
the appeal;

132
00:07:56.591 --> 00:08:00.160
I left home very young and spent many
years drifting around the country

133
00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:01.660
with a backpack and little else.

134
00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:05.160
What all of it left me with was a
permanent,

135
00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:08.860
low-grade suspicion of inherited authority
and received belief,

136
00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:12.160
which hardened from my early teens into
actual politics:

137
00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:16.160
some years as an activist with the
Communist Party of Australia

138
00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:18.900
and a longer time among anarchist groups.

139
00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:22.400
That is not a youthful embarrassment I
have outgrown.

140
00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:26.780
As the book will show, it is a thread that
runs straight into the argument,

141
00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.260
because the anarchist intuition

142
00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:31.200
that people are perfectly capable of
ordering their own affairs

143
00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.300
from the bottom up turns out,

144
00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:35.400
once you take the biology seriously,

145
00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:40.160
to be less a political fantasy than a plain
observation about a cooperative animal.

146
00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:46.880
Between that and Indonesia lies an
Australian working life I will pass over,

147
00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.620
save for the one strand that bears on this
book:

148
00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:52.620
somewhere in it I taught myself to
program,

149
00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.020
and spent a decade building the Australian
Labor

150
00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:57.700
Party's first computerised electorate
management,

151
00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.900
campaign and polling systems (yes, the
irony).

152
00:09:02.613 --> 00:09:06.140
In 1996 I moved -- migrated, actually --

153
00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:09.160
to Indonesia, and I have lived here ever
since.

154
00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:11.620
I am now an Indonesian citizen,

155
00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.180
and I make my living running a corporate
services firm (yes,

156
00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:20.000
another irony) and, increasingly, building
the knowledgebases this project draws on.

157
00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.840
That second irony deserves better than a
parenthesis.

158
00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:29.080
The moral centre of this book is a
community of Javanese peasants

159
00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:30.900
who refused the state's taxes,

160
00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.300
licences and schools;

161
00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:34.960
the man writing it has spent three decades

162
00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:37.620
selling safe passage through those very
toll-gates,

163
00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:39.360
incorporating foreign capital

164
00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.640
into the machinery the wong sikep simply
declined to enter.

165
00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:47.240
Apply to this book the question any old
communist would ask of anyone else's:

166
00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:48.560
who paid for the writing?

167
00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:50.820
Foreign direct investment did.

168
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.700
The firm financed the decades in-country,
the citizenship,

169
00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:58.160
the unhurried reading, and the servers the
knowledgebases run on.

170
00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:02.080
And the commute between those two worlds
ground the lens both ways.

171
00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.200
Three decades of processing what the state
actually demands of those

172
00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:09.820
who comply is a daily education in seeing
like a state,

173
00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:14.380
and it is exactly why the refusers impress
me and why I cannot romanticise them:

174
00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:18.960
a man who files the paperwork knows
precisely what declining it costs.

175
00:10:19.531 --> 00:10:21.300
But the distance is real too.

176
00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:25.980
The Sikep pay for their dharma in
schooling forgone and feet set in cement;

177
00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:29.100
I pay for mine in fees, invoiced monthly.

178
00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:34.220
I admire a refusal I have never had to
afford, and a reader should correct for that:

179
00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:37.420
expect this author to feel the romance of
exit more keenly

180
00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:39.260
than the quiet goods of compliance.

181
00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:44.420
What I will say for the day job is what a
pragmatist is entitled to say:

182
00:10:44.528 --> 00:10:48.460
I have to earn a living; and my firm gives
a living to hundreds of people,

183
00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:50.180
directly and indirectly;

184
00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:52.800
and the companies it has set up are real
businesses

185
00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:56.640
that have provided livelihoods to tens of
thousands of Indonesians.

186
00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:01.400
The contradiction, apparent or real, is
not one I have resolved.

187
00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:04.500
It is part of the workshop this lens was
ground in,

188
00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:06.500
and I put it on the record with the rest.

189
00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:12.640
Three decades inside Indonesian society
did the deepest work on the lens.

190
00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:16.960
I do not write about dharma as a Westerner
reaching for the exotic East.

191
00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:21.640
I live in a country whose language and
cosmology are saturated with Sanskrit,

192
00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:23.500
where dharma is an ordinary,

193
00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:26.640
slightly worn loanword rather than a
sacred mystery,

194
00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:29.140
and where the deep Indic substratum
beneath a

195
00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:32.600
nominally Muslim surface is never very far
from view.

196
00:11:32.999 --> 00:11:35.700
The word is furniture to me, not incense.

197
00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:39.700
That immersion had a particular shape.

198
00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:43.040
In my late thirties I went back to
university

199
00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:46.980
for a specialist degree in Indonesian
anthropology at Murdoch University,

200
00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:50.500
including a year at Universitas Gadjah
Mada in Yogyakarta,

201
00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:53.700
and it was there that my Asian Studies
lecturer, Paul Stange,

202
00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:57.540
a scholar of Javanese mysticism, brought
my attention to Sumarah:

203
00:11:57.700 --> 00:12:01.500
a quiet Javanese movement that calls
itself a philosophy of life,

204
00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:05.100
a practice of surrender with no fixed
guru, no creed,

205
00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:07.440
and no demand that you believe anything at
all.

206
00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:09.420
It was inevitably bound up

207
00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:13.420
with elements of traditional Javanese
culture I did not always share,

208
00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.300
but it gave me something I had no name for
at the time:

209
00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:18.600
a working example of a contemplative

210
00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:23.680
and ethical path carried within a culture
rather than a church, mosque or temple.

211
00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:28.480
This was, I can see now, my first
recognisable "secular dharma",

212
00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:32.600
met fully two decades before I ever sat
down in a Bodhi College retreat;

213
00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.480
and I am no detached observer,

214
00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:39.500
I built their international movement's
English-language website back in 1998.

215
00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:43.100
It planted early the suspicion I will
later argue:

216
00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:47.480
that a dharma is far harder to prise away
from its culture than from its gods.

217
00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:54.400
My personal life has been large and
disorderly by any conventional measure:

218
00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:56.160
more marriages than is decent,

219
00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:59.940
children and grandchildren scattered
across three countries,

220
00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:04.360
the ordinary share of estrangements and an
extraordinary share of complications.

221
00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:08.660
In 2012 I lost a son, and in 2015 a
partner,

222
00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:11.620
and it was during and after that stretch
that a long,

223
00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:15.260
idle interest in dharmas turned into
something closer to a need.

224
00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:18.780
I will not pretend to know whether grief
sharpened the search

225
00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:20.600
or merely cleared the time for it.

226
00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:23.220
But I would be misrepresenting the work

227
00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:25.520
if I let you picture it arriving out of
serenity.

228
00:13:26.571 --> 00:13:30.400
It came, like most things worth having,
out of a fair amount of mess.

229
00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:36.100
One more disclosure belongs here, because
it is printed on the cover.

230
00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:40.340
Biksu is the Indonesian word for a
Buddhist monk, and I am not one:

231
00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:43.960
no ordination, no robe, no teacher who
ever sanctioned me.

232
00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:46.100
Nor did I choose the name.

233
00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:49.840
It was given to me, beginning more than a
decade ago,

234
00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:52.180
by Indonesian friends and colleagues --

235
00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:54.440
all of them, as it happens, Muslims --

236
00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:57.940
who knew that I meditated, knew the kind
of thing I read,

237
00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:01.440
and watched me withdraw into my office
studios in Jakarta,

238
00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:04.420
Batam and Bali for weeks and months at a
time,

239
00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:09.580
running the firm entirely online, avoiding
direct contact even with its own clients,

240
00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:12.920
doing little but programming and studying
anthropology.

241
00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:16.980
The withdrawal deepened through the
grieving years just described,

242
00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:20.560
and also after the Jakarta terrorist
attack of 2016.

243
00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:22.760
Three bombs exploded,

244
00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:25.020
and the second and largest of those went
off in

245
00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:27.700
the Starbucks directly below my office
windows,

246
00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:30.200
where I had bought my coffee every working
morning.

247
00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:32.480
It was probably meant for me.

248
00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:36.960
The name, then, was teasing and serious at
once,

249
00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:39.240
in the way of most Indonesian nicknames.

250
00:14:39.851 --> 00:14:41.940
At first it sort of annoyed me.

251
00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.260
But given names have a way of sinking in,

252
00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:49.080
and over the years I have accepted it as
part of my Indonesian identity:

253
00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:53.380
my practice, and my company, Okusi, welded
into a single title.

254
00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:59.460
There is also a second, plainer reason
this name sits on this book.

255
00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:03.260
The pages ahead have things to say about
communism and atheism.

256
00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:08.360
In Indonesia, where every citizen's
identity card must state a religion,

257
00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:11.760
both words still carry real legal and
social weight.

258
00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:15.440
Publishing under this given name deflects
a little of

259
00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:17.540
that attention away from my legal one.

260
00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:19.920
It is a screen, not a mask;

261
00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:23.740
my legal name is attached to this
project's public repository

262
00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:26.100
and to everything else I have ever
published.

263
00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:30.560
The one idea

264
00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:37.200
If I were forced to compress what this
book argues into a single sentence,

265
00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:41.560
it would be this: a dharma is not a
revelation but a technology,

266
00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:43.940
an evolved cultural technology,

267
00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:46.820
worked out by a particular kind of social
animal

268
00:15:47.003 --> 00:15:49.500
to solve a particular and permanent
problem.

269
00:15:51.763 --> 00:15:53.940
The main problem is cooperation.

270
00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:57.140
Human beings get far more done together
than apart,

271
00:15:57.300 --> 00:15:59.540
but every cooperative group has a weak
spot:

272
00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:04.480
the cheat, the free-rider, the one who
takes the benefit and skips the cost.

273
00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:06.380
A species that found a way

274
00:16:06.380 --> 00:16:11.020
to hold cooperation together against its
own parasites would inherit the earth,

275
00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:13.260
and ours, more or less, did.

276
00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:17.680
We evolved, slowly and through both our
genes and our cultures,

277
00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:19.420
a thick toolkit for the job.

278
00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:24.040
A dharma, on this reading, is simply a
culture's particular,

279
00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:26.300
elaborated version of that toolkit,

280
00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:28.540
its worked-out answer to the questions of
how

281
00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:32.120
to live together and what holds the group
steady while it does.

282
00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:35.660
From the Vedas down to the working rules
of a profession,

283
00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:40.000
they are all running the same evolved
programme on different cultural hardware.

284
00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:42.880
From the inside, a dharma is more than
that;

285
00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:46.040
from the outside, it is the lens this
project tests.

286
00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:51.980
This is not an idea I invented, so I
should name the people I took it from,

287
00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:55.860
because they are the real intellectual
scaffolding of everything that follows.

288
00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:59.280
The frame leans on the human behavioural
biology of Robert Sapolsky;

289
00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:01.800
on Christopher Boehm's account of how
foraging

290
00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:04.340
bands actively hold their own equality in
place;

291
00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:05.700
on Sarah Hrdy's argument

292
00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:08.700
that cooperative child-rearing was the
cradle of human kindness;

293
00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:10.460
on David Sloan Wilson's case

294
00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:13.300
for selection acting on groups and not
only individuals;

295
00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:17.700
and, reaching back more than a century, on
the Russian anarchist Peter Kropotkin,

296
00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:21.440
whose Mutual Aid argued, against the
social Darwinists of his day,

297
00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:25.080
that cooperation is as natural a force in
evolution as competition.

298
00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:27.860
Set Stephen Batchelor's secular dharma
beside

299
00:17:27.860 --> 00:17:30.540
David Graeber's anthropology of mutual
aid,

300
00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:33.700
and with Sapolsky's human behavioural
biology underneath,

301
00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:35.220
and they stop looking like neighbours

302
00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:38.900
and start looking like the same thing said
in three vocabularies.

303
00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:45.460
I should flag, here and once, that this is
a particular and partial lens,

304
00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:47.380
and the book leans the way it leans:

305
00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:51.940
secular, evolutionary, broadly Western,
and a bit academic,

306
00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:54.500
even where the subject matter is none of
those things.

307
00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:58.920
In each part, I have tried to show where
that slant does the work

308
00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:01.560
and to look for the voices the lens tends
to miss.

309
00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:05.980
Show the workings

310
00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:11.560
This is the part I most want the reader to
understand,

311
00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:14.480
because it is the part that should earn or
lose your trust.

312
00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:19.500
For about forty years I have been a bit of
a magpie.

313
00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:24.480
Books, articles, transcripts, talks, my
own scribbled notes:

314
00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:28.380
a great unsorted pile of material about
anthropology,

315
00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:31.760
biology, politics, religion and the human
animal,

316
00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:35.220
accumulated long before I had any clear
idea what it was for.

317
00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:37.460
All I had was a magpie's faith

318
00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:41.760
that these found objects would one day fit
together into coherent montages.

319
00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:48.520
In 2020, when large language models ("AI")
first became capable enough

320
00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:50.100
for me to put to real work,

321
00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:51.680
I finally found the use.

322
00:18:52.390 --> 00:18:55.260
I began turning the pile into something
you could question,

323
00:18:55.600 --> 00:19:00.140
using an AI technique called
retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG.

324
00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:02.260
The idea is simple:

325
00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:05.720
instead of asking an AI to answer out of
the fog of its training

326
00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:07.620
and hoping it invents nothing,

327
00:19:07.640 --> 00:19:11.420
you keep a corpus of real, sourced
documents to one side;

328
00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:13.080
when a question comes in,

329
00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:16.780
the system first retrieves the most
relevant passages from that corpus,

330
00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:21.460
and only then asks the model to compose an
answer from those specific passages.

331
00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:24.840
The machine is given far less room to make
things up,

332
00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:27.760
and an obligation to show you where each
thing came from.

333
00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:34.100
That pile is now a knowledgebase of over
15,000 curated documents,

334
00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:37.280
articles, transcripts, and complete books,

335
00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:40.020
spanning anthropology in its widest sense,

336
00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:43.080
which I file under the name "applied
anthropology";

337
00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.880
secular dharma is one room inside that
larger house.

338
00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:50.360
Nor is the collection entirely my own
hoarding:

339
00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:53.720
an important part of it was supplied by
Irfan Kortschak,

340
00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:55.720
an Indonesia-based anthropologist

341
00:19:55.760 --> 00:20:00.100
who helped identify some core source texts
and contributed his own writings.

342
00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:02.600
It is the same body of material

343
00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:06.600
that the secular-dharma conversations were
folded into, transcribed,

344
00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:08.960
and cleaned, so that the talks Rupert,

345
00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:12.320
Elfie and I recorded are now part of the
searchable corpus.

346
00:20:14.110 --> 00:20:17.140
The book was built from that corpus in two
deliberate,

347
00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:20.500
separated stages, and the separation is
the whole point.

348
00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:23.820
In the first stage, I did not write prose
at all.

349
00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:27.300
For each question worth asking about
dharma,

350
00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:31.600
I did the research and produced a single,
heavily sourced research note:

351
00:20:31.780 --> 00:20:34.660
a piece of pure evidence-gathering whose
only job was

352
00:20:34.660 --> 00:20:39.000
to establish what could defensibly be said
and to attach a source to every claim.

353
00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:41.100
The notes are not graceful.

354
00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:43.560
They are the working,

355
00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:46.800
and a claim that could not be sourced did
not earn a place in them.

356
00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:50.740
Only in the second stage, and only once
the notes existed,

357
00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:52.660
were the parts written from the notes.

358
00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:56.960
The prose you are about to read therefore
stands on a body of evidence assembled

359
00:20:56.960 --> 00:20:58.860
before a word of it was composed,

360
00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:02.440
which is the exact reverse of the usual
essayist's habit of reaching

361
00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:06.020
for a supporting quotation once the
argument has already been decided.

362
00:21:07.820 --> 00:21:10.040
The discipline underneath all of this is
sourcing,

363
00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:12.320
and I have come to picture it through an
unlikely model.

364
00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:15.240
The classical Islamic scholars,

365
00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:20.180
faced with deciding which reported sayings
(hadith) of the Prophet could be trusted,

366
00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:22.620
built an exacting science around the
isnad,

367
00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:27.200
the chain of transmission: a report was
only as sound as the unbroken,

368
00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:30.180
named chain of people who had passed it
down,

369
00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:34.040
and a claim with a broken or doubtful
chain was flagged or thrown out.

370
00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:36.240
I am no Islamic scholar,

371
00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:40.180
but I have spent enough years in a Muslim
country to have absorbed the instinct,

372
00:21:40.280 --> 00:21:43.620
and it is this instinct that this project
tries to run on.

373
00:21:44.300 --> 00:21:47.120
Every substantive claim in this book
traces back,

374
00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:50.740
through the research notes, to a source
you could go and check for yourself.

375
00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:53.760
The chains themselves live in those
research notes,

376
00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:56.700
and every part ends with links to the
notes it stands on.

377
00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:59.040
You are not asked to trust me.

378
00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:01.360
You are asked to follow the links.

379
00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:07.100
Now the part that might make some readers
uneasy, so I will be exact about it.

380
00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:11.460
Artificial intelligence is built into the
foundations of this project,

381
00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:13.480
and without it the work could not exist;

382
00:22:13.580 --> 00:22:16.420
not because a machine had anything to say
about dharma,

383
00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:18.800
but because only a machine could make
forty

384
00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:21.660
years of accumulated reading instantly
answerable.

385
00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:25.760
Its work was the research: fetching
passages from the corpus,

386
00:22:25.760 --> 00:22:27.280
assembling the sourced notes,

387
00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:31.520
helping check citations against their
sources, flagging any broken chain.

388
00:22:32.020 --> 00:22:35.760
It is very good at that legwork, and it
made no final judgement;

389
00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:39.560
but its retrieval did shape what got
surfaced and what stayed buried,

390
00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:43.260
which is a bias with consequences that
Part 8 reckons with squarely,

391
00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:46.040
and it is why every chain was checked by
hand.

392
00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:49.560
A machine cannot know what is true or what
is worth saying,

393
00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:53.280
and a book is nothing but those
judgements, made one after another.

394
00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:57.380
The writing is mine: the structure and the
sentences you are reading,

395
00:22:57.380 --> 00:22:59.080
worked up from the research notes.

396
00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:01.520
Where the machine offered me text,

397
00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:06.500
I treated it as I treated every other
source: argued with it, overruled it,

398
00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:09.100
and caught it confabulating with fluent
confidence,

399
00:23:09.220 --> 00:23:13.400
so no claim was let stand until I had
traced it back to a real source myself.

400
00:23:13.940 --> 00:23:16.960
The voice is mine; so are the errors and
deficiencies.

401
00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:20.040
What I got in exchange is sourcing far
more thorough

402
00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:21.920
than one writer could manage alone.

403
00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:28.220
One last thing needs naming, because it
sits at the centre of the whole undertaking.

404
00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:34.740
The applied anthropology corpus is both my
instrument and, increasingly, my subject.

405
00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:39.120
Part 8 asks whether a machine could be
taught human ethics at all,

406
00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:41.060
and what would be lost in the teaching.

407
00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:44.480
I am, in a small and literal way,

408
00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:47.780
already running that experiment, feeding a
particular,

409
00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:52.880
plural, deeply human tradition into machines,
and then watching what it makes of it.

410
00:23:53.620 --> 00:23:57.880
I am not a neutral observer of that
question; I'm standing inside it.

411
00:24:00.980 --> 00:24:02.840
What this is, and isn't

412
00:24:05.330 --> 00:24:07.540
I hold no lineage and no authority,

413
00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:10.240
and there is no practice waiting at the
end of this book

414
00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:12.060
that I am surreptitiously selling.

415
00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:14.840
Nor is it academic anthropology,

416
00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:18.320
though it leans on a great deal of it: the
sourcing is real,

417
00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:20.160
but the voice is a personal one,

418
00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:23.960
written for an intelligent general reader
rather than for a subfield.

419
00:24:24.500 --> 00:24:27.000
What it is, is one person's attempt to
look hard

420
00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:29.180
and straight at a single enormous
question:

421
00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:32.800
what these things called dharmas are,
where they come from,

422
00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:36.480
and how they get made, with the workings
kept on show the whole way.

423
00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:40.840
The one commitment that runs through every
part,

424
00:24:40.840 --> 00:24:42.920
and the one I would ask you to hold onto,

425
00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:45.520
is a refusal to rank the dharmas in
advance.

426
00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:49.080
Secular and sacred, written and unwritten,

427
00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:53.260
the great world religions and the code of
a single profession or subculture:

428
00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:56.480
I treat them all as real candidates for
the same description,

429
00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:00.700
and decline to hand any of them the high
ground before the looking is done.

430
00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:04.860
This refusal is not woolly relativism,

431
00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:09.140
and I will try to show why some dharmas
genuinely fall through the floor.

432
00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:11.280
But it is the starting posture,

433
00:25:11.540 --> 00:25:14.640
and it emerges out of the most basic fact
about the word,

434
00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:17.720
which is that there was never a single the
dharma to begin with.

435
00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:22.600
There are only dharmas, plural, and Part 1
begins exactly there,

436
00:25:22.600 --> 00:25:27.180
with a word that almost no one can define
and almost everyone uses anyway.

437
00:25:28.930 --> 00:25:33.660
So: that is where all this comes from, who
helped make it, and how it was built.

438
00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:36.020
The throat-clearing is over.

439
00:25:37.840 --> 00:25:41.840
Biksu Okusi, August 2026, Bali, Indonesia

